terms.tex:
Fix a language collection $\cL = \{L_1, L_2, \dots\}$.
            The collection $\cL$ is said to satisfy Angluin's condition if for any index $i$, there is a tell-tale, \ie{}, a finite set of strings $T_i$ such that $T_i$ is a subset of $L_i$, \ie{}, $T_i\subseteq L_i$, and the following holds:
            \begin{center}
                \centering
                For all $j\geq 1$, if $L_j\supseteq T_i$, then $L_j$ is not a proper subset of $L_i$.
            \end{center}

quotes.tex:
Fix any countable collection of languages $\cL$. {$\cL$ satisfies Angluin's condition if and only if one of the following two equivalent conditions hold}
\begin{itemize}[itemsep=-2pt]
    \item[$\triangleright$] There is a stable algorithm that generates with approximate breadth from $\cL$.
    \item[$\triangleright$] There is a stable algorithm that generates with exact breadth from $\cL$.
\end{itemize}
